<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-proline degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PROUT-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: PRO| can be catabolized to |FRAME: GLT| by the action of two enzymatic activities: |FRAME: EC-1.5.5.2| (PDH) (sometimes referred to as proline oxidase) and |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.88| (previously known as &amp;Delta;-pyrroline-5-carboxylate dehydrogenase, or P5CDH). Whereas in eukaryotes PDH and P5CDH are encoded by two different genes, in most bacteria, including enteric bacteria |CITS: [6270100][6270101]|, |FRAME: TAX-1061| |CITS: [7592417]|, |FRAME: TAX-375| |CITS: [8572700]|, |FRAME: TAX-658| |CITS: [8645272]|, |FRAME: TAX-358| |CITS: [8971722]|, and |FRAME: ORG-6005| |CITS: [10715000]|, both steps are catalyzed by a single polypeptide encoded by the &lt;i&gt;putA&lt;/i&gt; gene. The enzyme is highly conserved among different microorganisms, but its genetic organization and control of expression are highly divergent |CITS: [10715000]|. |FRAME:TAX-4932| is able to utilize proline as a sole nitrogen source. |FRAME: PRO| is catabolized within the mitochondrial matrix. Since both enzymes are encoded in the nucleus and synthesized in the cytoplasm, they are imported into the mitochondria before they become active |CITS: [387737] [6358862]|. |FRAME: PRO| can serve as a total source of carbon and energy or of nitrogen for |FRAME:TAX-562|. In the two steps (both of which are catalyzed by a product of &lt;i&gt;putA&lt;/i&gt;) shown here, proline is converted to |FRAME: GLT|, which is further degraded to |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE|, an intermediate of the |FRAME: TCA|. Curiously, |FRAME: GLT|, the product of |FRAME: PRO| degradation, cannot itself serve as a total source of carbon and energy for |FRAME:TAX-562| because |FRAME: GLT| transport supplies exogenous |FRAME: GLT| at an inadequate rate. The pathway is shown here in three steps because |FRAME: L-GLUTAMATE_GAMMA-SEMIALDEHYDE| is an intermediate between |FRAME: L-DELTA1-PYRROLINE_5-CARBOXYLATE| and |FRAME: GLT|. The hydrolysis of |FRAME: L-DELTA1-PYRROLINE_5-CARBOXYLATE| to |FRAME: L-GLUTAMATE_GAMMA-SEMIALDEHYDE| occurs spontaneously. In addition to serving as a building block of proteins, |FRAME: PRO| acts as an osmotic protectant. Consistent with this role, high osmolarity inhibits |FRAME: PRO| degradation.</Description>
